An event is usable where a function is expected, and it still emits.

`tick` alone names nothing: an event is not a value, and `tick()` is an emit
recognised in call position. So a component handing its own event to something
that takes a callback had to write `func() { tick() }`, which says the same
thing twice and says it in a form the reader decodes before seeing that the
callback *is* the event. html's timer override, whose mount is
`setInterval(tick, d)`, is the case that asked for it.

The sugar produces an ir.Emit, which is what substituteEvents replaces with the
block the call site wrote -- so `direct` and `wrapped` below have to emit the
same code, and this golden is where that is visible. `carries` forwards a
payload, which is the only other shape accepted: a callback taking nothing emits
with no arguments, one taking a single value forwards it, and anything else is
an error against the event (see error_event_as_func_shape.sngl).

Asserted here rather than as a `sngl test` fixture because the interpreter
cannot invoke a funcvar -- `run(func() { … })` never calls its argument there,
with or without an event involved -- so an interpreter fixture for a callback
would pass while measuring nothing. Every compiled target emits it correctly,
which is what this pins.

No `#id` on the text, deliberately: an element ref read from inside a lambda in
an effect body emits the program-written name rather than the variable html
declared, so the page throws `out is not defined`. That predates this and is
visible on main with no event in sight -- pinning it here would bless it.

deny out/none/html/index.html `func\(\) \{ *fired` -- the wrapper is gone; the event is the callback
deny out/none/html/index.html `emit\(` -- an emit is substituted away, never emitted

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

func run(f func()) {
    f()
}

func runWith(f func(int)) {
    f(7)
}

component direct(@fired) node {
    effect(@mount {
        run(fired)
    })
}

component wrapped(@fired) node {
    effect(@mount {
        run(func() {
            fired()
        })
    })
}

component carries(@fired int) node {
    effect(@mount {
        runWith(fired)
    })
}

component main node {
    var (
        a = 0
        b = 0
        seen = 0
    )

    direct(@fired { a += 1 })
    wrapped(@fired { b += 1 })
    carries(@fired(v) { seen = v })
    text(value="{a}:{b}:{seen}")
}

window {
    main
}
-- out/none/html/index.html --
